[[Propaganda]] is a form of [[communication]] that aims to shape people's beliefs, actions and behaviours. It is generally not [[Impartiality|impartial]], and is hence viewed as a means of [[persuasion]]. It is often biased, misleading, or even false to promote a specific agenda or perspective. Propagandists use various techniques to manipulate people's opinions, including selective presentation of facts, the omission of relevant information, and the use of emotionally charged language. Propaganda has been widely used throughout history for largely financial, military as well as political purposes, with mixed outcomes.

Propaganda can take many forms, including [[political speech]]es, [[Advertising|advertisements]], news reports, and social media posts. Its goal is usually to influence people's attitudes and behaviors, either by promoting a particular ideology or by persuading them to take a specific action. The term propaganda has acquired a strongly negative connotation by association with its most manipulative and [[Jingoism|jingoistic]] examples.

{{Short description|Marxist theory of history and society}}
{{For|the journal|Historical Materialism (journal){{!}}''Historical Materialism'' (journal)}}
{{use Oxford spelling|date=April 2018}}
{{use dmy dates|date=April 2018}}
[[File:Historical Materialism.svg|thumb|upright=1.15|Diagram illustrating the theory of historical materialism]]
{{Marxism sidebar}}
'''Historical materialism''' is a theory of [[history]] and [[sociology]] in [[Marxism|Marxist]] thought that posits that material and economic conditions are the primary drivers of societal structure and historical change. First articulated by [[Karl Marx]] and [[Friedrich Engels]] in the mid-19th century, the theory proposes that the [[mode of production]] of material life—the way a society produces and reproduces the means of human existence—determines or conditions its political, legal, and ideological "[[Base and superstructure|superstructure]]". Historical development is understood as a succession of modes of production, with transitions between them driven by contradictions that arise between the developing [[Productive forces|forces of production]] (technology, [[labour power]]) and the static [[relations of production]] (class structure, property ownership).

The [[materialist]] conception of history was developed by Marx as a critical response to [[German idealism|German idealist]] philosophy, the ahistorical materialism of the [[Age of Enlightenment|Enlightenment]], and the [[classical political economy]] of thinkers like [[Adam Smith]] and [[David Ricardo]]. In what is considered its definitive formulation, from his 1859 preface to ''[[A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy]]'', Marx summarized the theory's guiding principle: "It is not the consciousness of men that determines their existence, but their social existence that determines their consciousness." Marx and Engels first sketched the theory in ''[[The German Ideology]]'' (1846), conceptualizing history as a series of "progressive epochs"—the [[Asiatic mode of production|Asiatic]], ancient, [[Feudalism|feudal]], and modern [[Bourgeoisie|bourgeois]] ([[Capitalist mode of production|capitalist]]) modes of production. Rather than a complete and established scientific theory, Marx and Engels presented these ideas as a set of methodological principles and hypotheses to serve as a "guiding thread" for historical and social research.

Following Marx's death, Engels expanded on these ideas in works such as ''[[Anti-Dühring]]'' (1878) and ''[[The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State]]'' (1884). According to some scholars, Engels's later formulations shifted the theory's emphasis from a critical-philosophical method to a [[Positivism|positivist]] and [[Determinism|deterministic]] science, presenting historical materialism as a specific application of a universal "[[dialectical materialism]]" that governed both nature and society. This interpretation became a cornerstone of [[Marxism–Leninism]] and Soviet orthodoxy, although it diverged from Marx's own approach, which was primarily concerned with the analysis of societal development, particularly the rise of capitalism from within European feudalism.

As a framework for analysis, historical materialism treats [[Class struggle|class conflict]] as the central mechanism of historical change, with [[Social revolution|social revolutions]] occurring when the existing relations of production begin to [[fetter]] the development of productive forces. While the theory has been criticized for its purported [[economic determinism]], and many of its core tenets have been subject to radical revision, its concepts have had a profound influence on [[sociology]], [[historiography]], and [[critical theory]].

{{Short description|Approach to biology}}
'''Feminist biology''' is an approach to [[biology]] that is concerned with the influence of gender values, the removal of [[gender bias]], and the understanding of the overall role of social values in biological research and practices.<ref name="sep">{{Cite SEP|url-id=feminist-philosophy-biology|title=Feminist Philosophy of Biology|first=Carla|last=Fehr|date=June 22, 2011}}</ref> [[Feminism|Feminist]] biology was founded by, among others, [[Ruth Bleier]] of the [[University of Wisconsin–Madison]] (who authored the 1984 work ''Science and Gender: A Critique of Biology and Its Theories on Women'' and inspired the university's endowed fellowship for feminist biology).<ref>{{cite web |first1=David |last1=Tenembaum |title=First in the nation: UW-Madison establishes post-doc in feminist biology |url=https://news.wisc.edu/first-in-the-nation-uw-madison-establishes-post-doc-in-feminist-biology/ |website=UW-Madison News |publisher=University of Wisconsin-Madison |access-date=17 April 2017 |language=en |date=17 April 2014}}</ref> It aims to enhance biology by incorporating feminist critique in matters varying from the mechanisms of cell biology and sex selection to the assessment of the meaning of words such as [[sex and gender distinction|"gender" and "sex"]].
Overall, the field is broadly defined and pertains itself to philosophies behind both [[Philosophy of biology|biological]] and [[Feminist philosophy|feminist]] practice (see also [[feminist philosophy of science]]). These considerations make feminist biology debatable and conflictive with itself, particularly when concerning matters of [[biological determinism]], whereby descriptive sex terms of [[male]] and [[female]] are intrinsically confining, or extreme [[postmodernism]], whereby the body is viewed more as a social construct.<ref>Dominguez, Johnna. "A Fine Line: Feminism and Biology." Club Antro: UA Anthropology Blog Network. University of Alabama, 27 Aug. 2012. Web.</ref> Despite opinions ranging from determinist to postmodernist, however, [[biologist]]s, feminists, and feminist biologists of varying labels alike have made claims to the utility of applying feminist ideology to biological practice and procedure.
{{Feminism sidebar}}

{{Short description|Technology and software to provide legal services}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=August 2024}}
{{Law sidebar}}'''Legal technology''', also known as '''legal tech''', refers to the use of technology and software to provide [[lawyer#Responsibilities|legal services]] and support the legal industry.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Whalen |first1=Ryan |title=Defining legal technology and its implications |journal=International Journal of Law and Information Technology |date=15 April 2022 |volume=30 |issue=1 |pages=47–67 |doi=10.1093/ijlit/eaac005|doi-access=free }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Mania |first1=Karolina |title=Legal Technology: Assessment of the Legal Tech Industry's Potential |journal=Journal of the Knowledge Economy |date=June 2023 |volume=14 |issue=2 |pages=595–619 |doi=10.1007/s13132-022-00924-z|doi-access=free }}</ref> Legal technology encompasses the use of traditional software architecture and web technologies, such as searchable databases of case law and other legal authority,<ref>{{Cite web|title=AustLII – User Tools: Sino Free Text Search Engine|url=https://www.austlii.edu.au/techlib/software/sino/|access-date=2021-09-26|website=www.austlii.edu.au}}</ref> as well as [[machine learning]] technologies, such as those used to automatically search documents for purposes of [[due diligence]] or [[Discovery (law)|discovery]].<ref name=":1" />{{Rp|1329}} Work on making contracts more easy to use involve aspects of [[user experience design]],<ref name=":2" />{{Rp|69}} and artificial intelligence.<ref name="abatech">{{cite web |last1=Fuentes |first1=Lourdes M. |title=Lessons from Legal Research's Past for the GenAI-Powered Legal Technology of Tomorrow |url=https://www.americanbar.org/groups/law_practice/resources/law-technology-today/2024/lessons-from-legal-researchs-past-for-the-genai-powered-legal-technology-of-tomorrow/ |website=ABA |publisher=American Bar Association |access-date=19 February 2025 |language=en}}</ref>
Alternative legal service providers (ALSPs) increasingly use legal technology to deliver transactional legal work at scale, helping in-house legal teams improve service delivery, streamline operations, and redirect capacity to higher-value tasks.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.legaldive.com/news/crowdstrike-cybersecurity-factor-alsp-alternative-legal-services-provider-acc-value-champion/649063/|title=How CrowdStrike’s Use of Factor Shifted Legal Team Mindset|website=Legal Dive|access-date=2025-07-09}}</ref>

{{Short description|Term used for certain metallic elements}}
{{About|dense metallic elements|toxic metals|Metal toxicity|other uses|Heavy metal (disambiguation)}}
{{Cite check|date=August 2024|reason=Checking of some of the sources indicated that many were incorrect, so everything needs to be checked.}}
[[File:Osmium crystals.jpg|thumb|upright=1.2|Crystals of [[osmium]], a heavy metal nearly twice as dense as [[lead]]<ref>{{harvnb|Emsley|2011|pp=288; 374}}</ref>]]
{{Sidebar periodic table|image=no|expanded=othersets}}

'''Heavy metals''' is a controversial and ambiguous term{{sfn|Duffus|2002}} for [[metal]]lic elements with relatively high [[density|densities]], [[atomic weight]]s, or [[atomic number]]s. The criteria used, and whether [[metalloid]]s are included, vary depending on the author and context, and arguably, the term "heavy metal" should be avoided.<ref name="PourretBollinger2021">{{Cite journal|last1=Pourret|first1=Olivier|last2=Bollinger|first2=Jean-Claude|last3=Hursthouse|first3=Andrew|date=2021|title=Heavy metal: a misused term?|journal=Acta Geochimica|volume=40|issue=3|pages=466–471|doi=10.1007/s11631-021-00468-0|bibcode=2021AcGch..40..466P|s2cid=232342843|url=https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-03174937/file/Heavy%20Metal%20March%202021.pdf|archive-date=2022-06-26|access-date=2022-06-01|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220626164019/https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-03174937/file/Heavy%20Metal%20March%202021.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{harvnb|Hübner|Astin|Herbert|2010}}</ref> A heavy metal may be defined on the basis of density, atomic number, or [[chemical property|chemical behaviour]]. More specific definitions have been published, none of which has been widely accepted. The definitions surveyed in this article encompass up to 96 of the 118 known [[chemical element]]s; only [[mercury (element)|mercury]], [[lead]], and [[bismuth]] meet all of them. Despite this lack of agreement, the term (plural or singular) is widely used in science. A density of more than 5&nbsp;g/cm<sup>3</sup> is sometimes quoted as a commonly used criterion and is used in the body of this article.

The earliest known metals—common metals such as [[iron]], [[copper]], and [[tin]], and precious metals such as [[silver]], [[gold]], and [[platinum]]—are heavy metals. From 1809 onward, [[light metal]]s, such as [[magnesium]], [[aluminium]], and [[titanium]], were discovered, as well as less well-known heavy metals, including [[gallium]], [[thallium]], and [[hafnium]].

Some heavy metals are either essential nutrients (typically iron, [[cobalt]], copper, and [[zinc]]), or relatively harmless (such as [[ruthenium]], silver, and [[indium]]), but can be toxic in larger amounts or certain forms. Other heavy metals, such as [[arsenic]], [[cadmium]], mercury, and lead, are highly poisonous.<ref>{{Citation |last=Tchounwou |first=Paul B. |chapter=Heavy Metal Toxicity and the Environment |date=2012 |title=Molecular, Clinical and Environmental Toxicology |volume=101 |pages=133–164 |editor-last=Luch |editor-first=Andreas |chapter-url=http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-7643-8340-4_6 |access-date=2026-04-19 |place=Basel |publisher=Springer Basel |language=en |doi=10.1007/978-3-7643-8340-4_6 |isbn=978-3-7643-8339-8 |pmc=4144270 |pmid=22945569 |last2=Yedjou |first2=Clement G. |last3=Patlolla |first3=Anita K. |last4=Sutton |first4=Dwayne J.}}</ref> Potential sources of heavy-metal poisoning include [[mining]],<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Rastanina N.K., Golubev D.A., Perfiliev A.V., Rastanin P., Popadyev I.A. |title=Assessment of the elemental status of the young population in Solnechny, Khabarovsk krai, as part of mining environmental monitoring |journal=Mining Science and Technology (Russia) |date=2025 |volume=10 |issue=2 |pages=161–168 |doi=10.17073/2500-0632-2024-11-338|doi-access=free }}</ref> [[tailings]], [[smelting]], [[industrial waste]], [[agricultural runoff]], [[chemical hazard|occupational exposure]], [[Environmental impact of paint|paints]], and [[treated wood|treated timber]].

Physical and chemical characterisations of heavy metals need to be treated with caution, as the metals involved are not always consistently defined. Heavy metals, as well as being relatively dense, tend to be less [[chemical reaction|reactive]] than lighter metals, and have far fewer [[solubility|soluble]] [[sulfide]]s and [[hydroxide]]s. While distinguishing a heavy metal such as [[tungsten]] from a lighter metal such as [[sodium]] is relatively easy, a few heavy metals, such as zinc, mercury, and lead, have some of the characteristics of lighter metals, and lighter metals, such as [[beryllium]], [[scandium]], and titanium, have some of the characteristics of heavier metals.

Heavy metals are relatively rare in the [[structure of the Earth#Crust|Earth's crust]], but are present in many aspects of modern life. They are used in, for example, [[golf club]]s, [[cars]], [[antiseptic]]s, [[self-cleaning oven]]s, [[plastic]]s, [[solar panel]]s, [[mobile phone]]s, and [[particle accelerator]]s.

{{Short description|Short man-made radio signals}}
{{About|the fast radio burst signal||Peryton (disambiguation)}}
[[File:Pertyon.png|thumb|A peryton event detected at the [[Parkes Observatory]].]]

In [[radio astronomy]], '''perytons''' are short man-made [[radio signal|radio signals]] of a few milliseconds resembling [[fast radio burst|fast radio bursts]] (FRB). A peryton differs from radio frequency interference by the fact that it is a pulse of several to tens of millisecond duration which sweeps down in frequency. They are further verified by the fact that they occur at the same time in many beams, indicating that they come from Earth, whereas FRBs occur in only one or two of the beams, indicating that they are of galactic origin. <ref>{{Cite web |title=Peryton Research {{!}} CaltechMicrowave2.org |url=https://www.caltechmicrowave2.org/peryton-research |access-date=2023-03-28 |website=caltechmicrowave |language=en}}</ref> The first signal occurred in 2001 but was not discovered until 2007. First detected at the [[Parkes Observatory]], data gathered by the telescope also suggested the source was local. <ref name="RBWESCSTO"/><ref name="Hall 2015">{{cite web |last=Hall |first=S. |url=http://www.skyandtelescope.com/astronomy-news/microwave-ovens-spark-radio-signals-peryton-05122015/ |title=Microwave Ovens Spark Radio Signals |work=[[Sky & Telescope]] |date=May 12, 2015 |accessdate=2015-12-25 |quote=The reason dates back 17 years, when astronomers first spotted fleeting bursts of radio signals in their data. }}</ref> The signals were found to be caused by the premature opening of a [[microwave oven]] door nearby.

