'''''Music''''' is a 2021 American [[Musical film|musical]] [[Drama (film and television)|drama]] film directed by singer-songwriter [[Sia]]. The film was co-written by Sia and [[Dallas Clayton]], and stars [[Kate Hudson]], [[Leslie Odom Jr.]] and [[Maddie Ziegler]]. It marks Sia's feature film directorial debut. The film follows Zu, a newly sober drug dealer who becomes the sole guardian of her half-sister Music, a teenage [[Nonverbal autism | nonverbal]] [[autistic]] girl.<ref>Sharf, Zack. [https://variety.com/2022/film/news/sia-suicidal-rehab-music-outrage-1235157745 "Sia Reveals 'I Was Suicidal' and 'Went to Rehab' After Backlash to Controversial ''Music'' Film"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220920163308/https://variety.com/2022/film/news/sia-suicidal-rehab-music-outrage-1235157745/ |date=September 20, 2022 }}, ''Variety'', January 19, 2022</ref>

The film began development following Sia's announcement of the project in 2015. Originally intended as a non-musical film, it was subsequently re-conceived as a musical with a larger budget, and was filmed in mid-2017. A [[Music – Songs from and Inspired by the Motion Picture|studio album by Sia]] was also released in connection with the film, including some of the 10 songs she wrote for it.

''Music'' was released in Australia on January 14, 2021, by [[StudioCanal]], and in select [[IMAX]] theatres in the United States for one night on February 10, 2021, followed by an on-demand release on February 12, 2021, by [[Vertical Entertainment]]. The film was a [[box-office bomb|commercial failure]], grossing $645,949 against its $16 million budget, and received largely unfavorable reviews from critics, with most criticism stemming from its depiction of autism. It was nominated for [[Golden Globe Award for Best Motion Picture – Musical or Comedy|Best Motion Picture – Musical or Comedy]] and [[Golden Globe Award for Best Actress – Motion Picture Comedy or Musical|Best Actress – Motion Picture Comedy or Musical]] (Hudson) at the [[78th Golden Globe Awards]], but was also nominated for four [[41st Golden Raspberry Awards|Golden Raspberry Awards]], winning [[Golden Raspberry Award for Worst Actress|Worst Actress]] for Hudson, [[Golden Raspberry Award for Worst Supporting Actress|Worst Supporting Actress]] for Ziegler and [[Golden Raspberry Award for Worst Director|Worst Director]] for Sia.

